\begin{abstract}
The Astro-Ontological Computational Species Continuity Engine is a unified,
privacy-preserving evolutionary simulation architecture that integrates
multi-messenger astrophysical data streams with encrypted computational
organism state evolution. The system combines Gaia DR3 astrometry, JWST
spectroscopy, and LIGO gravitational wave strain signals into high-dimensional
trait vectors, evolving computational organisms under astrophysical influence
fields. Species boundaries are determined using a Multispecies Coalescent Model
(MSC), while all trait-space updates are executed under Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song
(CKKS) fully homomorphic encryption. Zero-knowledge Groth16 proofs verify
lineage continuity without revealing underlying state vectors. This manuscript
presents the full scientific validation suite, empirical benchmarks, and
cryptographic soundness analysis demonstrating the engine's correctness,
precision, and reproducibility across heterogeneous hardware environments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Multi-messenger astrophysics provides a rich, high-dimensional observational
basis for modeling cosmic environments. Simultaneously, computational species
delimitation offers a mathematically rigorous framework for tracking lineage
divergence. The Astro-Ontological Computational Species Continuity Engine
integrates these domains into a single encrypted evolutionary simulation
pipeline. The system evaluates organism trait evolution directly on encrypted
vectors, ensuring privacy-preserving computation across distributed nodes.

